# REGRESSION — the alias-spelled view defect reproduces with a LITERAL
# payload, not only a heap-forced one.
#
# CELL: payload heap-ness = string LITERAL (`v.push("hello")`). Every other
# coordinate matches `cow_alias_spelled_view_survives_root_growth.gg`:
# 1-hop alias · view via alias, mutation via root · bare-local root ·
# element type String.
#
# This fixture exists because the filed repro's header asserted, in bold,
# that "fewer pushes OR a literal payload is rc 0 on both spellings and
# proves nothing". Only the first half is true. Measured on the pre-fix
# compiler: this program is rc 139 SIGSEGV on BOTH backends and its control
# (the view spelled `v.get(0)`) is rc 0 -- so a literal payload reproduces
# the defect AND preserves the spelling discrimination. The realloc count is
# the load-bearing condition; the payload's heap-ness is not.
#
# The distinction matters because the two payloads take different runtime
# paths: a literal element is a static `*_materialize`, a `mk()` result is a
# heap-owned value. Both borrow out of the SAME reallocating array buffer,
# which is what the defect turns on.

void main():
    Vector[String] v = []
    v.push("hello")
    Vector[String] alias = v
    String s = alias.get(0).unwrap()
    int i = 0
    while i < 64:
        v.push("filler")
        i = i + 1
    print(s)
